{"name":"Prairie Winds","hex":"#e8e6d9","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"prairie","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An extensive area of relatively flat grassland with few, if any, trees, especially in North America."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/prairie"},{"term":"winds","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["plural of wind"]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["third-person singular simple present indicative of wind"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/winds"}],"entries":[{"title":"Prairie","blocks":[{"text":"Prairies are ecosystems considered part of the temperate grasslands, savannas, and shrublands biome by ecologists, based on similar temperate climates, moderate rainfall, and a composition of grasses, herbs, and shrubs, rather than trees, as the dominant vegetation type. Temperate grassland regions include the Pampas of Argentina, Brazil, and Uruguay, and the steppe of Romania, Ukraine, Russia, and Kazakhstan. Lands typically referred to as \"prairie\" tend to be in North America. The term encompasses the lower and mid-latitude of the area referred to as the Interior Plains of Canada, the United States, and Mexico. It includes all of the Great Plains as well as the wetter, hillier land to the east. From west to east, generally the drier expanse of shortgrass prairie gives way to mixed grass prairie and ultimately the richer and wetter soils of the tallgrass prairie."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b4/James_Woodworth_Prairie.jpg/330px-James_Woodworth_Prairie.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Prairie","wikidata":"Q194281"},{"title":"Wind","blocks":[{"text":"Wind is the natural movement of air or other gases relative to a planet's surface. Winds occur on a range of scales, from thunderstorm flows lasting tens of minutes, to local breezes generated by heating of land surfaces and lasting a few hours, to global winds resulting from the difference in absorption of solar energy between the climate zones on Earth. The study of wind is known as anemology."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e2/Cherry_tree_moving_in_the_wind_1.gif/330px-Cherry_tree_moving_in_the_wind_1.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wind","wikidata":"Q8094"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/94/Wildflowers_on_Blackland_Prairie.jpg/500px-Wildflowers_on_Blackland_Prairie.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/94/Wildflowers_on_Blackland_Prairie.jpg/1280px-Wildflowers_on_Blackland_Prairie.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/94/Wildflowers_on_Blackland_Prairie.jpg/500px-Wildflowers_on_Blackland_Prairie.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AWildflowers_on_Blackland_Prairie.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6e/WY_Redds_Great_plains.jpg/500px-WY_Redds_Great_plains.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6e/WY_Redds_Great_plains.jpg/1280px-WY_Redds_Great_plains.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6e/WY_Redds_Great_plains.jpg/500px-WY_Redds_Great_plains.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AWY_Redds_Great_plains.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4f/Great_plains_storm_-_Great_Sand_Dunes_National_Park.jpg/500px-